  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2 over x, z in [-2, 2], drawn at half height. It is steeper along x, so overshoot starts there: steps grow past learning rate 2/3 (about 0.67).",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with mean 0 and SD 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)): 2 at batch 1, exactly 0 at batch 64.",
    "Both arrows are drawn 2× the step they show. Orange is the next mini-batch step (the way the ball will move); grey is −learning rate × true slope. The readout gives the true step length.",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished), or as soon as the loss goes past 8 or the ball leaves the drawn square (diverged). Off-bowl positions are capped on screen; the loss readout stays true.",
    "Noise comes from fixed seed 7, indexed by run and step, so Reset replays the same noise. New run uses the next noise values. The ball takes 5 steps a second at normal speed.",
    "Each trail holds up to 40 points: where the ball lands after each step. The start point is not part of the trail."
  ]
}